      "notes": "[Highlight] Facebook had its strongest engagement month yet - views up 27.5% to 70.3K and viewer reach up 33.9%, with content interactions up 19.3%. The reach gain is translating into real engagement, not just passive impressions.\n\n[Watch] Blended CPL rose to $12.10 (from $9.39 in July), driven almost entirely by the Trade in AED Offer - Victoria campaign, where cost per lead jumped to $44.48. EasyPort remains the most efficient lead source at $5.49 CPL, and FRED PA-1 held fairly steady at $11.10. Worth reviewing Trade in AED targeting and creative, or pausing it, if the trend continues into September.\n\n[Instagram] A quieter month across the board - reach, interactions, clicks and visits all down double digits. Fresh creative ahead of Shocktober would help reset momentum here.\n\n[Follows] Facebook follows dropped sharply (down 60% to 2) - small numbers so easy to over-read, but worth a light follow call-to-action in September content.\n\n[September - FRED PA-1] Building interest in the FRED PA-1 ahead of Shocktober in October. Three angles to work into content and ads:<br><br>1. <strong>The defibrillator you buy once</strong> - lead with the 10-year warranty (one of the longest in Australia) as reassurance and value for workplaces and clubs weighing up cost, rather than a spec-sheet feature.<br><br>2. <strong>Not advertised as portable. But it lives in the ute anyway.</strong> - real-world carry use cases (tradies, sporting clubs, family road trips) leaning into the glovebox and car angle without ever calling it \"compact.\"<br><br>3. <strong>A soft bridge into Shocktober</strong> - a short educational piece on why a well-maintained AED matters, tied back to the warranty story, rather than a hard sell straight away."
